    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Portuguese founded Rio in 1565 after driving out a French colony on an island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanabara-bay">Guanabara Bay</a>, and the city became the colonial capital in 1763, when the administration moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>.</li><li>In 1808 the Portuguese royal court fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion and settled in Rio, which stayed the capital through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Empire of Brazil</a> and the Republic until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brasilia">Brasília</a> replaced it in 1960.</li><li>Rio was a city of enslaved people: between 1811 and 1831 half a million to a million enslaved people arrived through the Valongo Wharf, and its port was the largest slave port in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When the Portuguese court arrived in 1808, Rio became the only European capital outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and many of its inhabitants were evicted from their homes to house the arriving nobles.</p></aside>
    </section>

